   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="address">
                    <ab>
                        <address>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI_30">7 rue Edmond Valentin</name>
                            </addrLine>
                            <addrLine>
                                <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">
                                    <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI">Paris</name> 7<hi rend="superscript">e</hi>
                                </seg>
                            </addrLine>
                        </address>
                    </ab>
                </div>
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>With best wishes for the New Year to yourself and family from us both</p>
                    <closer>
                        <signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">James Joyce</name>
                        </signed>
                        <dateline>
                            <date when="1935-12-30">30.xii.935</date>
                        </dateline>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="global-note1" type="global">
                    <p>The face of this postcard reproduces a painting, <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">La Répétition de Danse</name>, by Edgar Degas (1834-1917). An oil painting on canvas, it was executed circa 1874, and is one of many scenes of dancers and dance classes, rehearsals, and performances that Degas drew, painted, or sculpted (http://www.degas-catalogue.com/the-rehearsal-214.html). The painting is often called, in English, <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">The Rehearsal</name> or <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">The Spiral Staircase</name>. It was owned at one time by  Jacques-Émile Blanche (1861-1942), the French artist who painted well-known portraits of JJ in the 1930s. It was later acquired, as part of the Burrell Collection, by the Glasgow Art Galleries and Museums. The photograph used on this postcard dates from the time the painting was on loan to the Tate Gallery, Millbank, London, in 1924, when the Tate was still called the National Gallery of British Art.</p>
                    <p>The postcard was produced by Waterlow &amp; Sons, Limited, of London, founded in 1810 as high-quality printers of “fine lithographic items such as posters, especially for the London subway, and postcards for institutions such as the British Museum.” The company was sold in 1961 (http://www.metropostcard.com/publishersw.html).</p>
                </note>
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">JJ and his wife <name type="person" key="barn84">Nora</name> moved into a flat at 7 rue Edmond Valentin in Paris in February 1935 and remained there until April 1939. It was on the fifth floor, near the river and not far from the Eiffel Tower. “It had five rooms, a telephone, and heat, and it could be reached by elevator” (Ellmann, <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">JJ</name> 674). Pound had trouble reading this address in JJ’s purblind scrawl, and wrote him in 1937: “why the HELL dont you PRINT yr/ damn address, ef a sick chicken tried to cross that road it’d end up as a unknown sojer. My Jayce; rue Eddie whichwott. Paris, basta” (Robert Spoo, “Unpublished Letters of Ezra Pound to James, Nora, and Stanislaus Joyce,” <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">James Joyce Quarterly</name>, vol. 32, no. 3-4 [Spring-Summer 1995]: 570).</note>
            </div>
      </body>
